96,4185,49109,320,91289,8,315,220,16,21,15,323,220,16,18,24,24,1447,16,13,17767,220,16,18,24,24,284,220,23,1124,50853,220,16,21,15,488,220,16,20,24,1124,340,17,13,17767,220,16,21,15,284,220,16,1124,50853,220,16,20,24,488,220,16,1124,340,18,13,17767,220,16,20,24,284,220,16,20,24,1124,50853,220,16,488,220,15,1124,3623,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nNotice that  \\[35\\cdot40=1400.\\]Find some integer $n$ with $0\\leq n<1399$ such that $n$ is the multiplicative inverse to 160 modulo 1399.\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThis means we need to find \\( n \\) such that \\( 160n - 1399k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 159 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 1 \\cdot 159 + 1 \\)\n3. \\( 159 = 159 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"c453dc5efccf3083cad5f5df954ebfd2fef20c39376826eaec2698ecc307ba22"},"seconds":18.55379319190979,"seed":507544976179589968,"stop":"eos","text":"The Euclidean Algorithm stops here, and we find that the gcd is 1, confirming that 160 and 1399 are coprime, and an inverse exists.\n\nNow, we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399:\n\nStarting from step 2:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\]\n\nFrom step 1, we know:\n\\[ 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\nSubstituting for 159 in the previous equation, we get:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) \\]\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1 \\cdot 1399 \\]\n\nThus, the coefficient of 160, which is 9, is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399. Therefore, the integer \\( n \\) we are looking for is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{9}\\)"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{9}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":true},"graded_text":"The Euclidean Algorithm stops here, and we find that the gcd is 1, confirming that 160 and 1399 are coprime, and an inverse exists.\n\nNow, we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399:\n\nStarting from step 2:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\]\n\nFrom step 1, we know:\n\\[ 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\nSubstituting for 159 in the previous equation, we get:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) \\]\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1 \\cdot 1399 \\]\n\nThus, the coefficient of 160, which is 9, is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399. Therefore, the integer \\( n \\) we are looking for is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{9}\\)","id":"number_theory:231"}
